The compute-smoke and gemm-sweep runners (runners/compute-smoke,
runners/gemm-sweep) link NVIDIA CUTLASS, a header-only BSD-3-Clause template
library. It is compiled into each runner binary; no CUTLASS source is
redistributed by this project. The gemm-sweep build asserts the licence from the
LICENSE.txt of the exact commit fetched. The CUTLASS subdirectory python/CuTeDSL
is covered by the NVIDIA EULA rather than BSD-3-Clause and is NOT used here.

Third-party components built into the other runner images, each verified against
the project allowlist. Every one is built from source in a build stage; see each
runner's Dockerfile and README for the per-image assertion that enforces this.

  runners/clockprobe
    - clockprobe.cu, nvml_dynamic.h   Apache-2.0, this project's own source.
      NVML is reached by dlopen against the driver's libnvidia-ml.so.1 at
      runtime; no NVIDIA header (including nvml.h) is vendored or redistributed.

  runners/dcgm-diag
    - NO DCGM IS SHIPPED IN THIS IMAGE. The image contains only our own
      Apache-2.0 wrapper on a distroless base; the site supplies DCGM at run
      time (the wrapper's PATH and LD_LIBRARY_PATH lead to the documented
      /usr/local/dcgm mount point), which is how DCGM already reaches GPU
      nodes.

      The DCGM source (v4.2.3) is cloned at build time only, to assert field
      ids against dcgm_fields.h; nothing from that clone is redistributed.

      This entry previously claimed DCGM was "Apache-2.0, built from source"
      with "its LICENSE travelling in the image". That was wrong on both
      counts and is corrected here. DCGM's *source* is Apache-2.0, but its
      *binaries* are not: every DCGM package in NVIDIA's distribution —
      including datacenter-gpu-manager-4-core, the one built from that
      Apache-2.0 source — carries the NVIDIA Data Center GPU Manager License
      in its copyright file. Section 2(c) of that licence forbids
      distribution, and section 2(e) forbids use that would subject it to a
      licence requiring free redistribution. Shipping those binaries from an
      Apache-2.0 repository would breach both the licence and this project's
      permissive-only policy. The NGC image additionally installs
      cuda-cudart and cuda-compat, which are NVIDIA-redistributable and
      barred by the runner-image rule in CLAUDE.md.

      Building DCGM from source was evaluated and rejected as impractical
      rather than merely inconvenient: upstream's build.sh re-invokes itself
      inside NVIDIA's own container via intodocker.sh (docker-in-docker) and
      requires a crosstool-NG toolchain at /opt/cross plus eleven staged
      dependencies. Even if it built, the resulting binaries would carry the
      licence above.

